C = [
("h1", "2. The Cross in the Gospel Paul Preached"),
("p", "Claim: the first thing Paul says he delivered, in the gospel he preached at Corinth, is that Christ died for our sins. He also says that he determined to know nothing among them “save Jesus Christ, and him crucified.” The death of Christ is therefore not an addition to Paul’s gospel. It stands at the front of it. But the sentence is short, and it says less than later systems say about it. This chapter reads what it says."),
("sub", "2.1 “First of all” (1 Corinthians 15:1–4)"),
("p", "“Moreover, brethren, I declare unto you the gospel which I preached unto you, which also ye have received, and wherein ye stand” (1 Corinthians 15:1). Paul then says what he delivered: “For I delivered unto you first of all that which I also received, how that Christ died for our sins according to the scriptures; And that he was buried, and that he rose again the third day according to the scriptures” (1 Corinthians 15:3, 4). Three things are named: that Christ died for our sins, that he was buried, and that he rose again the third day. The death and the resurrection are each said to be “according to the scriptures.”"),
("ctbl", "What 1 Corinthians 15:3–4 says and what it leaves unsaid.", [
    ("The clause", "What it says", "What it leaves unsaid"),
    ("“Christ died for our sins”", "Christ died, and the death is on behalf of sins; “our” is Paul’s and the Corinthians’", "How the death deals with sins; the sentence gives no figure"),
    ("“according to the scriptures”", "The death was foretold in the Scriptures", "Which Scriptures; the sentence names none"),
    ("“and that he was buried”", "A burial followed the death", "Nothing about the grave’s meaning here"),
    ("“rose again the third day according to the scriptures”", "The resurrection is the third element, on the third day", "How the resurrection and the death are related; Romans 4:25 says something of it (chapter 4)"),
  ], [0.28, 0.36, 0.36]),
("p", "The verse is a statement of what was delivered, not an explanation of it. It says that the death is “for our sins.” It does not say, in that verse, that the death was a propitiation, or a curse, or an offering. Those figures are given elsewhere, in other letters, for other needs. The sentence of 1 Corinthians 15:3 is the front door of the doctrine, and the chapters that follow go through the rooms."),
("sub", "2.2 “The preaching of the cross” (1 Corinthians 1:17–25)"),
("p", "Paul writes in the first chapter of the same letter that “Christ sent me not to baptize, but to preach the gospel: not with wisdom of words, lest the cross of Christ should be made of none effect” (1 Corinthians 1:17). He then says: “For the preaching of the cross is to them that perish foolishness; but unto us which are saved it is the power of God” (1 Corinthians 1:18). The cross is the subject of preaching. It is received in two ways by two sorts of hearers: those who perish count it foolishness, and those who are saved find it the power of God."),
("p", "He adds: “But we preach Christ crucified, unto the Jews a stumblingblock, and unto the Greeks foolishness; But unto them which are called, both Jews and Greeks, Christ the power of God, and the wisdom of God” (1 Corinthians 1:23, 24). The sentence is about how Paul’s own message was received by different hearers. It says that the Jews found the crucified Christ a stumblingblock, and the Greeks found him foolishness, and that those who were called, from both, found him power and wisdom. It does not say that Paul preached one message to Jews and another to Greeks. It says that he preached one thing, Christ crucified, and that it met two kinds of resistance."),
("sub", "2.3 “Save Jesus Christ, and him crucified” (1 Corinthians 2:1–2)"),
("p", "“And I, brethren, when I came to you, came not with excellency of speech or of wisdom, declaring unto you the testimony of God. For I determined not to know any thing among you, save Jesus Christ, and him crucified” (1 Corinthians 2:1, 2). The sentence describes Paul’s determination at Corinth. It does not say that Paul knew nothing else, or taught nothing else to the believers there, and the rest of the letter shows that he taught a great deal. It says what he determined to make the centre of what he declared when he first came."),
("sub", "2.4 What the believing is said to do"),
("p", "The chapter gives the Cross its place in the gospel. It does not yet say what believing does with it, and the passages say little in the same sentences. “By which also ye are saved, if ye keep in memory what I preached unto you, unless ye have believed in vain” (1 Corinthians 15:2) puts salvation with the gospel that was preached. “It pleased God by the foolishness of preaching to save them that believe” (1 Corinthians 1:21) puts it with believing. The volume takes both as written. The Body is saved by believing the gospel Paul preached, and the first thing in that gospel is that Christ died for our sins. The volume does not say, here or anywhere, that people who lived before this gospel was preached were saved by believing it. What earlier believers believed is the subject of other books, and this one is about Paul’s letters."),
("sub", "2.5 Where the chapter stops"),
("p", "Four things the chapter does not do. It does not say what the Greek word behind “for” in “for our sins” means beyond what the English says. It does not say that “according to the scriptures” names any single passage. It does not say that the preaching of the cross at Corinth was the whole of what Paul preached there. And it does not use the preaching of the Cross in the book of Acts, which is the subject of the Acts volumes of this press. The volume reads the letters."),
]
